Subject: [PATCH] Add refresh_logp_grad to AdaptiveWalnuts and WalnutsSampler

Both classes cache the log density and gradient at their current position from
one transition to the next, so a client that changes the target between
transitions scores the next transition's initial state under the previous
target. stan4bart is such a client: it draws the parametric conditional inside
a two-block Gibbs sampler, and the BART fit becomes the parametric offset (and,
for binary responses, the latents become the response) between every pair of
transitions. Left stale, the initial point's log density and gradient are a
sweep out of date and the chain is biased, not merely slowed. This adds one
method to each class that re-evaluates at the unchanged position, costing one
evaluation and touching nothing else. It is an upstream ask; until upstream
takes it, tools/vendor-walnuts.sh reapplies it on every re-vendoring.

---
diff --git a/walnuts/adaptive_walnuts.hpp b/walnuts/adaptive_walnuts.hpp
index 2eb43c8..e7fdc34 100644
--- a/walnuts/adaptive_walnuts.hpp
+++ b/walnuts/adaptive_walnuts.hpp
@@ -250,6 +250,17 @@ class AdaptiveWalnuts {
     ++iteration_;
   }
 
+  /**
+   * @brief Re-evaluate the log density and gradient at the current position.
+   *
+   * The cached log density and gradient carry over from the previous
+   * transition, so a client that mutates the target between transitions must
+   * call this before the next one; otherwise that transition's initial state
+   * is scored under the previous target. Costs one log density and gradient
+   * evaluation and leaves the position and every tuning value alone.
+   */
+  void refresh_logp_grad() { logp_grad_(theta_, logp_, grad_); }
+
   /**
    * @brief Return a Walnuts sampler with the current tuning parameter
    * estimates.
